 ## Approach
 
-Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. The approach for delivering committed changes is still open: five-second HTTPS polling, self-hosted WebSockets, or a hosted realtime service. All three can meet the required freshness target. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+Keep one authoritative board per event. Editors submit commands over HTTPS; browsers display a revisioned view of the board. Committed changes reach browsers over self-hosted WebSocket sessions: the server holds each browser's connection and pushes committed revisions as they land. Self-hosting gives fast updates without a recurring realtime vendor bill or provider coupling. It accepts connection-capacity, reconnect, and reliable-publication work that a hosted service would absorb.
 
-Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery must be designed together after this choice. Polling would favor cursor queries; self-hosted sockets would need connection management and replay; hosted delivery would need publication reconciliation and provider credentials. Neither operating cost nor minimum operational burden has been declared the overriding criterion.
+The two alternatives were rejected. Five-second HTTPS polling is the simplest stateless operation, but it repeats reads and leaves seconds of lag. A hosted realtime service reaches low latency with less connection operations, but adds recurring fees and provider coupling. Subsecond updates would be useful, but are not required.
+
+Delivery, change storage, and browser recovery are designed together around the socket approach. The change store retains replayable revisions so a reconnecting browser can catch up from its last-seen revision, and it publishes committed revisions reliably to the delivery layer. Delivery manages socket sessions and connection capacity. Browser sync subscribes, reconnects, and catches up from the last revision it received.
 
